  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0106 database is designed to store information about Formula 1 racing, encompassing various aspects of the sport, including circuits, constructors, drivers, and races. The database contains 14 tables, each representing a distinct entity within the racing world. The circuits table stores information about the locations where the races take place, including their unique identifier, reference, name, location, country, latitude, longitude, altitude, and URL. The constructors table holds details about the teams that participate in the racing, such as their identifier, reference, name, nationality, and URL. The drivers table contains information about the drivers, including their identifier, reference, number, code, forename, surname, date of birth, nationality, and URL. The races table stores information about the individual races, including their identifier, year, round, circuit identifier, name, date, time, and URL. The seasons table contains a list of years for which racing data is available, with each year serving as a primary key. The status table stores a list of possible statuses that a race can have, such as \"Finished\" or \"Not Started\". The results table holds the outcome of each race, including the identifier, race identifier, driver identifier, constructor identifier, grid position, finishing position, points earned, and other relevant details. The constructorResults table stores the points earned by each constructor in each race, while the driverStandings table stores the points earned by each driver in each race. The constructorStandings table stores the points earned by each constructor in each race, as well as their position and wins. The qualifying table stores the results of each qualifying session, including the identifier, race identifier, driver identifier, constructor identifier, and position. The lapTimes table stores the lap times for each driver in each race, including the race identifier, driver identifier, lap number, position, time, and milliseconds. The pitStops table stores the pit stop data for each driver in each race, including the race identifier, driver identifier, stop number, lap number, time, duration, and milliseconds. The target table stores the target data for each driver in each race, including the target identifier, race identifier, driver identifier, and win count. The database also includes several foreign keys to establish relationships between the tables, ensuring data consistency and integrity. For example, the constructorResults table references the constructors and races tables, indicating that the points earned by a constructor in a race are linked to the constructor and the race in which they were earned. Similarly, the results table references the constructors, drivers, and races tables, indicating that the outcome of a race is linked to the constructor, driver, and race involved. The database also includes several indexes and constraints to ensure data consistency and integrity, such as primary keys, unique constraints, and foreign key constraints with ON DELETE and ON UPDATE rules.",
  "gold_question": "Ergast F1 Historical Data Web Service\n\n 1) Background / Context\nErgast.com provides a comprehensive database of Formula 1 race data, starting from the 1950 season to the present. It includes detailed information about races, circuits, drivers, constructors, and their performances in various aspects such as lap times, pit stops, and qualifying rounds.\n\n 2) Objectives\nThe main objective of Ergast.com is to offer a rich set of historical data for Formula 1 enthusiasts, researchers, and developers through a web service interface. It aims to support data-driven analysis and provide an accessible platform for querying detailed F1 statistics.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Primary Users: Formula 1 analysts, data scientists, researchers, and developers interested in motorsports data.\n- Secondary Users: Formula 1 fans and historians seeking insights into historical race data.\n- Stakeholders: Motorsports organizations, educational institutions, and software developers using the data for applications or research.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n- Database Access: Users can query detailed information about races, circuits, drivers, constructors, lap times, pit stops, qualifying results, and standings.\n- Race Information: Access data on individual races, including year, round, location, and results.\n- Driver and Constructor Details: Retrieve performance details such as points, standings, and nationality.\n- Performance Metrics: Analyze lap times, pit stops, and qualifying times for drivers and constructors.\n- Historical Trends: Enable statistical analysis and trend identification over multiple seasons spanning several decades.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Performance: The database should efficiently handle and process complex queries, accommodating historical data spanning over 70 years.\n- Scalability: Design the system to support a growing dataset with ongoing Formula 1 seasons and increased user queries.\n- Accessibility: Provide a reliable and user-friendly API for accessing the dataset with comprehensive documentation for ease of use.\n- Security: Ensure secure data handling and access controls to protect the integrity and privacy of the dataset.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0106.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0106\nCREATE TABLE `circuits` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reference` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `latitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `longitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `altitude` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `constructors` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reference` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `nationality` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `drivers` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `reference` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `number` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `code` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `forename` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `surname` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_of_birth` date NOT NULL,\n  `nationality` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `races` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `round` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `circuit_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date` date NOT NULL,\n  `time` time DEFAULT NULL,\n  `url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`circuit_id`) REFERENCES `circuits` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `seasons` (\n  `year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`year`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `status` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `status` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `results` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `constructor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `grid` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `position` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `points` decimal(5,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`constructor_id`) REFERENCES `constructors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `constructorResults` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `constructor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `points` decimal(5,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`constructor_id`) REFERENCES `constructors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `driverStandings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `points` decimal(5,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `constructorStandings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `constructor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `position` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `wins` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`constructor_id`) REFERENCES `constructors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `qualifying` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `constructor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `position` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`constructor_id`) REFERENCES `constructors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `lapTimes` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lap` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `position` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `time` time NOT NULL,\n  `milliseconds` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `pitStops` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `stop_number` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lap` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `time` time NOT NULL,\n  `duration` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `milliseconds` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `target` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `race_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `driver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `win_count` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`race_id`) REFERENCES `races` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`driver_id`) REFERENCES `drivers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
